  "toast_tutorialtaskdone": "[color=#48ff00]Task completed:[/color]\r\n[color=#ffffff]{%TASK%}[/color]\n  ",
  "error_TrainingCharacterInvolved": "This action cannot be performed with or on an Agent in training.\n  ",
  "background_request_norobot": "No robot found\n  ",
  "background_request_getrobot": "REQUEST ARKHE\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_extensions_trigger01_desc": "The '''Agent profile''' window includes all the information and options connected to you. This is where you can track your financials, your relation to others, and much more.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_extensions_trigger02_desc": "One of the most important things you should keep an eye on are your '''extensions'''. These are the skills that enable you to use certain equipment or make you better at specific activities.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_extensions_trigger03_desc": "Extensions need '''extension points (EP)''' to install and upgrade - it is your most precious resource.\r\n\r\nThere are two ways to earn EP:\r\n\r\n1. The fastest way is by being active: destroying enemy robots, completing assignments, mining and harvesting, or taking part in manufacturing all provide various amounts of EP.\r\n\r\n2. You also receive a daily package of EP as long as you keep logging in at least every 3 days.\r\n\r\nYou can also purchase an '''EP booster''' which increases both the daily package and the EP coming from activities. Also, while the booster is active, you don't have to worry if you miss to log in for more than 3 days.\r\n\r\nIf you acquire enough EP for an extension, you can instantly install or upgrade it. The number of available extensions alone can be daunting at first - read their descriptions and only upgrade something if you already know what it does.\r\n\r\n[[Image:checklist_extensions.jpg|center|nolink]]\r\n''Tip: It may not be worth it to max out an extension. From level 1 to 5, the EP required for upgrading goes up linearly. However, from level 6 and up the EP cost increases exponentially. Level 10 costs almost as much as the first 9 levels altogether!''\r\n\r\nAn important aspect of your available EP pool is that it’s account-based: this means if you have more than one Agent, you have to spend EP on them from the '''same pool of points'''.\r\n\r\nFor the duration of this training all of your extensions have been set to level 5. Also, installing and upgrading extensions will not require any EP here, so you can experiment around as much as you like. (Alas, you will not keep them after your training ends.)\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_sparks_trigger01_desc": "Your spark, the tiny nanobot you control can be changed to different models.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_sparks_trigger02_desc": "[[Image:checklist_spark.jpg|center|nolink]]\r\nThere are quite a few types with different kind of '''bonuses'''. You can see what each of them does if you hover over their bonus icons.\r\n\r\nSome of the sparks are available to install right from the start, but for many you have to get the proper '''authorization''' before you can use them.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_repairshop_trigger01_desc": "Damaged robots need repairs and most of the items that you loot from destroyed robots will also be more or less damaged.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_repairshop_trigger02_desc": "[[Image:checklist_repairshop.jpg|right|nolink]]\r\nThe repair shop lists all the '''damaged items''' in your private storage and you can select those you want to repair for NIC.\r\n\r\n''Tip: you can also equip an armor repairer module on your robot to repair it when deployed.''\r\n\r\nFeel free to repair any damaged items you may have and then '''move on to the next task'''.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_market_trigger01_desc": "The market is the main platform for '''trading items'''. The currency we use here is called Nian Credits, or '''NIC''' for short.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_market_trigger02_desc": "Items are sorted into categories - selecting a category will list all its items below the category tree. Selecting an item will show the '''buy and sell adverts''' for it on the right.\r\n\r\nThere are two main methods of a market transaction:\r\n\r\n* '''Direct transactions:''' if there is immediate supply or demand for an item, buying or selling it for the specified price will complete instantly.\r\n* '''Adverts:''' if there is no current supply or demand, or if you want to undercut another seller, you can post an advert with your desired price and duration. The transaction will then complete whenever someone buys or sells to your advert.\r\n\r\n[[Image:checklist_market.jpg|right|nolink]]\r\nNote that '''markets are local'''. Supply and demand will vary from terminal to terminal, therefore trading between terminals can be a lucrative career as well. The markets in range tab will give you some hints about prices and availability around the world. (Also, the prices you see here are not representative of real-world prices, these are just for this training.)\r\n\r\nGo ahead and buy a '''Sequer'''. It’s a small transport robot, you can find it under the Robots/Industrial robots/Industrial assault robots category. You will need it for the next task.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_market_trigger03_desc": "A robot purchased on the market comes in boxed form, so you have to '''unpack''' it before you can use it.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_market_trigger04_desc": "Now that it’s unpacked it can be '''activated'''.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_assignments_trigger01_desc": "If you simply want to do “something” to earn some NIC, you can always turn to assignments, which provide you with various tasks within the category of your choice.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_assignments_trigger02_desc": "Assignments are laid out here in a grid, where columns mean '''assignment categories''' and rows mean difficulty '''levels'''. Here in this training we only have a level 0 transport category, but after your training ends you’ll be able to select combat and industrial categories and more difficult levels as well.\r\n\r\nBesides having monetary or sometimes item rewards, assignments also increase your '''relation''' towards the contractor corporation’s faction - TM, ICS, or ASI. These relation values are displayed on the top of the assignments window.\r\n\r\n[[Image:checklist_assignmentrequest.jpg|right|nolink]]\r\nHigher relation gives you '''bonuses''' in terminal facilities, and they also enable you to do higher level assignments with higher rewards. The limits are pretty straightforward here: 100 relation unlocks level 1 assignments, 200 unlocks level 2 and so on.\r\n\r\nThere is only one assignment to request here, so let’s do that now.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_assignments_trigger03_desc": "The '''assignment information''' window shows all the things you need to know: objectives (click their links to get a popup map), rewards, expiration time (if any), and a detailed description. You’ll also get an entry in your '''active assignments''' list, so you can keep track if you have multiple assignments ongoing.\r\n\r\nMake sure that the '''Sequer''' is still your active robot, and let’s deploy to the terrain.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_assignments_trigger04_desc": "There is also a window available here on the terrain which helps you track your assignments and their objectives.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_assignments_trigger05_desc": "You can keep track of the '''status of your objectives''' here on the terrain in this window: red means incomplete, green means complete. The letter icons of the objectives are also shown on the terrain and in the landmarks list just like any other object, so you can use them as navigation reference.\r\n\r\n[[Image:checklist_assignmentpickup.jpg|right|nolink]]\r\nNote that the objective icons are just location indicators. You can’t interact with them, you need to interact with the objects that are near them.\r\n\r\nSo this assignment is a quick '''pickup and delivery''' task, just to have a little taste of how things go - follow the objectives and complete the assignment. If the objectives are too vague for you, just bring up the assignment information window with the '''“info” button''' and the detailed description should help you out.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_assignments_trigger06_desc": "Nice work! For your next task you’ll need another robot so just enter the terminal to finish this one.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_scanningmining_trigger01_desc": "[[Image:checklist_argano.jpg|right|nolink]]\r\nAlthough the Syndicate provides some basic equipment ready to use, most of the robots and modules need to be '''produced''' by our Agents, with the help of various industrial facilities in the terminals. The most basic resources for industrial production are '''raw minerals''' - this is where mining comes in.\r\n\r\nWe have sent an '''Argano''' into your private storage. It’s a light industrial robot specialized for mining, but we’re also going to use it now for some harvesting and artifact hunting.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_scanningmining_trigger02_desc": "The robot is already equipped with everything we’ll need, so just deploy to the terrain.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_scanningmining_trigger03_desc": "You’ll need to use the Terminal teleport again, but this time choose the '''Industrial area''' destination.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_scanningmining_trigger04_desc": "Since the '''mineral fields''' are under the surface we need to use '''geoscanners''' to locate them. Geoscanner modules are pretty versatile: depending on the charge you load into them, they can be used for different scanning methods.\r\n\r\n[[Image:checklist_geoscannerammo.jpg|right|nolink]]\r\nThe first step now is to find the general location of a mineral field by using a method called '''directional scanning'''. Changing between different types of charges works almost like reloading a module: just right click the geoscanner module, open up the '''“Ammo/charge”''' submenu, and select the directional geoscanner charge.\r\n\r\nNote that there are specific geoscanner and miner charges for each type of mineral and you need to load the correct one, like in this case for Solibdit.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_scanningmining_trigger05_desc": "Wait until the module finishes loading and then activate it.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_scanningmining_trigger06_desc": "The directional scan does what its name suggests: it points you the direction towards the '''nearest mineral field''' of the scanned type, represented by the '''red arrow'''. The arrow doesn’t measure distance however, it only points towards the direction of the minerals, not the exact location.\r\n\r\n[[Image:checklist_directionalscan.jpg|right|nolink]]\r\nThe method to follow here is to go towards the direction of the arrow, do another scan, adjust your bearing towards the new arrow, and so on. Once you get close enough to the minerals, a '''red X''' will be shown instead of an arrow.\r\n\r\n''Tip: a smart scanning method is to do a scan, move sideways away from the arrow, and do another scan. The imaginary point where the two arrows would cross should be very close to your goal.''\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_scanningmining_trigger07_desc": "Nice. Now that we have the general location of the Solibdit field, it’s time to pull out some precision tools. '''Tile-based geoscanner charges''' work only with a relatively small coverage, but they show exactly which ground tiles have minerals and also how much of it.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_scanningmining_trigger08_desc": "Again, wait until loading is finished and activate your scanner.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_scanningmining_trigger09_desc": "[[Image:checklist_tilescan.jpg|right|nolink]]\r\nThe result of the scan is projected onto the terrain like a '''heat map'''. Blue-green means low, yellow is pretty good, and red means the largest amount of minerals.\r\n\r\nNote that your geoscanner module has an '''accuracy''' parameter. If this value is lower than 100%, the geoscan result will be more or less noisy, which makes it harder to spot tiles with low amounts of minerals. You can check your geoscanner’s accuracy by doing a rightclick-info on it.\r\n\r\n''Tip: accuracy can be increased by using higher tier equipment or upgrading the relevant extensions.''\r\n\r\nBefore you can start mining, you need to select a ground tile that has some minerals and '''lock it''' as a target. Go for a yellow or red tile if possible.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_scanningmining_trigger10_desc": "Your miner module should be already loaded with Solibdit miner charges, so just simply activate it to start mining the locked ground tile.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_scanningmining_trigger11_desc": "If you open up your cargo window now, you can see that it’s slowly being filled up with Solibdit.\r\n\r\nWe don’t need much of it though, so just '''deactivate''' your miner module and '''move on to the next task.'''\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_harvesting_trigger01_desc": "[[Image:checklist_harvest.jpg|right|nolink]]\r\nRaw materials can also be gathered from certain wild growing '''plants'''. If a ground tile has a plant on it, the landmark information panel will show that. The plants that can be harvested will show the amount they hold with a yellow number.\r\n\r\nContrary to mining, harvesting doesn’t need different charges for different plants - one charge fits all. There is also no scanning involved here, you just simply need to '''use your eyes''' to find some.\r\n\r\nThe plant we’re looking for now is one with purple-pink round growths called '''Helioptris'''.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_harvesting_trigger02_desc": "Activate your harvester module and it will start the gathering process.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_harvesting_trigger03_desc": "A few cycles is enough just to see how it works. Deactivate it and '''move on to the next task'''.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_artifacts_trigger01_desc": "[[Image:artifact_scanner.png|right|nolink]]\r\nAnother very handy feature of a geoscanner is that it can search for long-lost (or purposefully buried, who knows...) artifacts.\r\n\r\nArtifacts can contain scraps, ammo, industrial stuff, minerals, and even rare high-tech equipment. Beware that some of the artifacts are guarded and enemy robots will appear when you find them.\r\n\r\nThere are 3 kinds of '''artifact scanner charges''', with different scanning ranges: 1000, 3000, and 5000 meters. The higher the range, the more expensive the charge, so the strategy here is to start with high range and change to a lower one as soon as you get close enough of an '''artifact signal'''.\r\n\r\nFor this test we’ll be using the middle one, the R-30 charge.\n  ",
  "tutorialchecklist_advanced_artifacts_trigger02_desc": "Wait until loading is finished and then activate it.\n  ",
